ine.edition\n    FROM MAGAZINE Magazine\n    INNER JOIN MAGAZINE_DATA MagazineData on Magazine.id = MagazineData.id\n    WHERE Magazine.id in (%filteredIds%)\n    \n\n\n\n### Answer:\n\nOption 1 is always better. Your option 2 is a bad habit:\n\n  * You have to run three SQL statements instead of one\n\n  * Those long `IN` lists are cumbersome to create and process, and they can make SQL statements very long\n\nMoreover, there is no point in sorting the results of the first query, since\nthat ordering will be lost in the other queries, which require another `ORDER\nBY`. Finally, you\'d have to merge the two ordered lists.\n\n"""'}], 'response': "The root cause for such a long runtime is that the planner has rewritten the query embedding the view code in an inefficient way. \n\nTo modify the query and give results in acceptable times, you can use Common Table Expressions (CTEs) to force the planner to first compute (materialize) the selection from the views:\n\n```\nWITH \n   a AS MATERIALIZED  (SELECT name, population, place \n                FROM SH_POINT \n                WHERE place IN ('hamlet', 'city', 'town', 'village') ), \n   b AS MATERIALIZED  (SELECT name, population \n                FROM SH_POLY \n                WHERE boundary='administrative' AND admin_level='8' ) \n    \nSELECT a.name, a.population, a.place, b.population \n   FROM a, b \n   WHERE a.name = b.name;\n```\n\nThis query will first compute the selection from the views and then join them together. This should give you results in acceptable times.\n\nFor the second case, both queries are equivalent and would be reduced to the same thing by the optimiser. However, I prefer the second option (`SELECT id FROM tbl WHERE status NOT IN ('3','7','9')`) as it's shorter and thus easier to parse.\n\nFor the third case, you can improve the performance of your query by creating indexes on `group_id`, `attribute_id` and `int_value`. You can also insert all int_values into a temp table and then create the 